<h2 id="neither-mad-nor-a-thief">Neither Mad Nor a Thief</h2>

<p>One afternoon in the Capuchin friary at San Giovanni Rotondo, John McCaffery passed on to Padre Pio something he had heard from a Jesuit superior. The Jesuit had been told about the crowds that surrounded the old friar, and he had wondered aloud how anyone’s humility could survive them.</p>

<p>Padre Pio laughed. He laughed harder than McCaffery had ever seen him laugh, as if it were the best joke he had heard in his life. When he could speak again, he gave his friend a message to take back. He hoped, he said, that he was neither mad nor a thief, because a man who claimed for himself what belonged to God alone would have to be one or the other. Then he put it in a picture. Suppose someone handed you a fine gold watch to take to Milan for repair. And suppose that on the train you took it out and showed it to the other passengers as if it were your own. What would that make you? A fool, or something worse.</p>

<p>The watch he meant had been in his keeping for a long time. For fifty years Padre Pio bore wounds in his hands, his feet, and his side, the places where Christ was wounded on the Cross, and they bled. Doctors examined them and disagreed about what they were. A bishop sent by the Holy Office in Rome questioned him in his own friary. The Holy Office declared that nothing supernatural had been proved, and for about two years it forbade him to say Mass in public or to hear confessions. People said he could read their souls, that he had been seen in two places at once, and that a scent like violets hung in the air around him. And still they came, to a friary on the Gargano in the south of Italy, until by the time he died his prayer groups counted some sixty-eight thousand members in fifteen countries.</p>

<p>He had never wanted any of it to be seen. The first red marks appeared on his palms while he was a young priest living at home in his village. He asked the parish priest to pray with him that Jesus would take them away. He was willing to suffer, he is remembered to have said, even to die of suffering, “ma tutto nel nascondimento”: but all of it in hiding. What he wanted to be, he said, was only “un povero frate che prega,” a poor friar who prays.</p>

<p>The Catholic Church declared him a saint in 2002, and the pope who did it had met him as a student priest. Padre Pio wrote no books. He said Mass, he heard confessions, he wrote letters to his spiritual directors because they told him to, and in his later years he built a hospital. He asked to suffer in hiding, and he lived half a century in the open, under the eyes of crowds, doctors, and Vatican investigators. How he did it without once showing the watch as his own is the story of his life. It begins in a village in the province of Benevento, with a shepherd boy who thought that every child saw what he saw.</p>

<h2 id="the-boy-who-thought-everyone-saw">The Boy Who Thought Everyone Saw</h2>

<p>Francesco Forgione was born on 25 May 1887 in Pietrelcina, a village in the province of Benevento, and baptized the next day. His parents, Grazio Forgione and Maria Giuseppa De Nunzio, were peasants who worked a piece of land at a place called Piana Romana, outside the village. Neither of them could read, but they told their children the stories of the Bible, and every evening the family prayed the rosary together. Until he was about ten, Francesco’s work was minding the sheep.</p>

<p>The people who later wrote about his childhood recorded something stranger. From about the age of five, it was said, he saw Jesus and the Virgin Mary in the village church, and the Sacred Heart came to him at the altar rail. It did not strike him as unusual. He assumed that all children saw the same things.</p>

<p>At ten he came down with typhoid and recovered. In those years a Capuchin friar from the friary of Sant’Elia a Pianisi visited the district, and Francesco wanted to become a Capuchin, one of the Franciscan friars of the Capuchin reform. A friar needed more schooling than a shepherd boy had. So his father, Grazio, went to work in the United States, and the money he earned paid for a tutor. At twelve Francesco was confirmed and made his first Communion.</p>

<p>He was fifteen when the time came to leave. On New Year’s Day 1903, a few days before his departure, he received Communion and, as he later told it, was shown in a vision that his whole life would be a struggle with Satan. On the night of 5 January, his last at home, he saw God and the Virgin Mary encouraging him. The Church that declared him a saint sets one sentence of his at the head of his official life, and it reads like a summary of everything that lay ahead:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“The Lord showed me, as in a mirror, that my whole future life would be nothing but a martyrdom.”</p>
  <p><em>“Il Signore mi fece vedere, come in uno specchio, tutta la mia vita futura non essere altro che un martirio.”</em></p>
  <p><cite>— Padre Pio, quoted at the head of his biography by the Dicastery for the Causes of Saints (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>On 6 January 1903 he entered the Capuchin novitiate at Morcone. Within two years, the body that was supposed to carry him through that life began to fail.</p>

<h2 id="a-novice-who-could-not-eat">A Novice Who Could Not Eat</h2>

<p>On 22 January 1903, at Morcone, Francesco Forgione put on the Capuchin habit and received a new name, Fra Pio: Brother Pio. Four years later, on 27 January 1907, he bound himself to the order for life with solemn vows of poverty, chastity, and obedience. By the time he made them he was rarely well. Half a century later a pope would release him from one of the three, so that he could run a hospital.</p>

<p>From about 1905 his health gave way in one form after another. He lost his appetite, he fainted, he vomited, and he suffered from headaches and bronchitis; for long stretches the only food he could keep down was milk and cheese. In later years his lungs were blamed, and some spoke of tuberculosis. His superiors moved him to friaries in the mountains, and early in 1909 they sent him home to Pietrelcina. In the middle of these years one of his brother friars claimed to have seen him in ecstasy, raised above the ground.</p>

<p>One of the strangest stories ever told about him also begins in these years. According to a devotional account, in February 1905 the young friar wrote a note about something he had seen. He had found himself in Udine, at the other end of Italy, at the bedside of a dying father while a baby girl was being born. The girl, Giovanna Rizzani Boschi, had been born on 18 January 1905. Many years later she gave testimony in the process for his beatification.</p>

<p>Illness did not keep him from the altar. He was ordained a deacon at Morcone on 18 July 1909, and on 10 August 1910, in the chapel of the cathedral at Benevento, Archbishop Paolo Schinosi ordained him a priest. Four days later he celebrated his first solemn Mass in Pietrelcina. He was twenty-three. Because of his health, his superiors let him go on living at home in the habit. For most of the next six years he stayed there, saying Mass in the village and writing to his directors at the friary of San Marco in Lamis.</p>

<p>Out at Piana Romana, on the family land, his brother Michele had built him a little hut. After Mass the young priest would go out there to pray in the open air. It was there, he later said, that something began to happen to his hands.</p>

<h2 id="red-marks-the-size-of-a-coin">Red Marks the Size of a Coin</h2>

<p>The date he later gave was 7 September 1910, less than a month after his ordination. At the hut on Piana Romana, marks appeared in the young priest’s hands. For a year he put nothing about them in writing. When he finally told his spiritual director, in September 1911, he admitted that he had kept quiet out of “that cursed shame.” Then he described what he had found:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“In the middle of the palms of my hands a little red has appeared, about the size of a cent coin, with a strong, sharp pain.”</p>
  <p><em>“In mezzo al palmo delle mani è apparso un po’ di rosso, grande quanto la forma di un centesimo, accompagnato da un forte e acuto dolore.”</em></p>
  <p><cite>— Padre Pio, letter to his spiritual director, September 1911 (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>A month later, writing to Father Agostino, he went further. The marks would become visible, he wrote, especially in one hand, and prayer could take away the sign but not the pain. Almost every week, he added, he suffered the crowning with thorns and the scourging, the torments of Christ before the Cross.</p>

<p>He also confided in the parish priest of Pietrelcina, Don Salvatore Pannullo, known as Zi’ Tore, Uncle Tore. The words he is remembered to have said to him run through the rest of this story. “Zi’ Tore, fatemi la carità, chiediamo a Gesù che mi tolga questa confusione.” Uncle Tore, do me this kindness: let us ask Jesus to take this embarrassment away from me. “Voglio soffrire, morire di sofferenza, ma tutto nel nascondimento.” I want to suffer, to die of suffering, but all of it in hiding.</p>

<p>Don Salvatore did not promise to pray the marks away. If this was God’s will, he told the young priest, he would have to bend to it.</p>

<p>For seven years more the marks stayed, for the most part, out of sight, as he had prayed. The pain, by his own account, stayed with him. And the struggle that mattered most to him in those years was happening somewhere else, in the long letters he was sending to San Marco in Lamis.</p>

<h2 id="letters-to-san-marco-in-lamis">Letters to San Marco in Lamis</h2>

<p>In 1911, at the friary of Venafro, Padre Pio had been confined to his bed, and the friars there reported strange things around him. One of them later said that for more than twenty days he had lived on nothing but Holy Communion. From December 1911 until February 1916 he lived on sick leave in Pietrelcina, in the house of his brother Michele. His two spiritual directors, Father Agostino and Father Benedetto of San Marco in Lamis, asked him to write to them about his soul, and he obeyed. His letters to them from these years and after now fill the first of the four volumes of his collected correspondence, the <em>Epistolario</em>.</p>

<p>In them a young man tries to put into words what is happening between him and God. Describing one morning when he felt wholly united with Christ, he reached for a simple image:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“My heart had vanished, like a drop of water that loses itself in the sea.”</p>
  <p><em>“Il mio cuore era scomparso, come una goccia d’acqua che si smarrisce in un mare.”</em></p>
  <p><cite>— Padre Pio, letter to a spiritual director, <em>Epistolario</em> (1995 ed.), 273 (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Suffering, for him, was the other half of that love. “Gesù mi dice che nell’amore è lui che diletta me; nei dolori, invece, sono io che diletto lui,” he wrote. Jesus tells me that in love it is he who delights me, while in sorrows it is I who delight him. So he could write, “Voglio soffrire. È questa la mia brama.” I want to suffer. This is my longing.</p>

<p>There were also seasons when God seemed to have gone. “Io cerco Dio, ma dove rinvenirlo?” I look for God, but where am I to find him? At his lowest point he wrote, “Una sola cosa mi resta amica: la morte.” Only one thing is still my friend: death. And he fought a temptation that will surprise anyone who knows only the legend of the wonder-working friar. It was the wish to be admired. “Dio mio! che martirio è la tentazione della vanagloria,” he wrote. My God, what a martyrdom the temptation of vainglory is.</p>

<p>What held all of it together was the Cross. In one letter he recorded words that he said Jesus had spoken to him: “Sotto la Croce s’impara ad amare.” Under the Cross one learns to love. Calvary, as he put it, was “il monte dei Santi,” the mountain of the saints. For him the sufferings of body and soul were a way of staying close to Christ, and he urged the people he guided to stop fighting and let themselves be carried. “Abbandonatevi pienamente sul cuore divino di Gesù, come un bimbo tra le braccia della madre,” he would tell them. Give yourselves up wholly to the divine heart of Jesus, like a child in its mother’s arms.</p>

<p>He worried, too, about his fellow priests. In one vision, he wrote, Jesus showed him priests who displeased him, and cried out, “Macellai!” Butchers!</p>

<p>Not every line in these early letters is his own. Scholars comparing them with other texts have found that eleven letters he wrote to his directors between 1911 and 1913 copy long passages, word for word, from another writer. She was Gemma Galgani, an Italian stigmatic who was later declared a saint. For his critics, the borrowing raises the question of how much of his early account of himself he learned from her. It is one of the hard questions about the young Padre Pio, and it is part of his story.</p>

<p>In 1914 he began to write to someone outside the order as well, a noblewoman named Raffaelina Cerase, and their letters of 1914 and 1915 fill the second volume of the <em>Epistolario</em>. To her he wrote that it seemed Jesus had nothing else in hand but to sanctify her soul. On the feast of the Assumption that year he set out for her, in a few lines, the road he believed every chosen soul must walk. John Paul II quoted them on the day he declared Padre Pio a saint:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“To reach our final end we must follow the divine Head, who leads the chosen soul by no other road than the one he walked himself: the road of self-denial and the Cross.”</p>
  <p><em>“Per arrivare a raggiungere l’ultimo nostro fine bisogna seguire il divin Capo…”</em></p>
  <p><cite>— Padre Pio to Raffaelina Cerase, 15 August 1914, <em>Epistolario</em> II, p. 155 (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>He was still at home in Pietrelcina, ill and writing, when in November 1915 the army called him up.</p>

<h2 id="the-friary-on-the-gargano">The Friary on the Gargano</h2>

<p>Italy’s army could never keep him for long. Padre Pio was drafted on 6 November 1915 and posted in December to the Medical Corps in Naples, where he was sent home on sick leave, recalled, and sent home again, more than once. Not until 16 March 1918 did the army finally declare him unfit for service, with a double bronchial pneumonia.</p>

<p>Meanwhile his life as a friar had found its place. In February 1916 he left Pietrelcina, and on 28 July he went up for the first time to the Capuchin friary of Our Lady of Grace at San Giovanni Rotondo, a town on the Gargano. He stayed about a week. On 4 September 1916 he came back, this time to stay, and he never left. He would live there for fifty-two years.</p>

<p>The friary kept a school for boys who hoped to become Capuchins, the seraphic seminary, and the new arrival became its spiritual director. He was also guiding a growing circle of women by letter, his spiritual daughters, and his letters to them from 1915 to 1923 would one day fill a third volume of the <em>Epistolario</em>. He was hearing the boys’ confessions on 5 August 1918 when, he wrote afterward, a heavenly figure appeared before him holding something like a long iron blade, with fire at its point. The figure drove it into his soul. He told the boy in front of him to leave.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“I could barely let out a moan; I felt I was dying.”</p>
  <p><em>“A stento emisi un lamento; mi sentivo morire.”</em></p>
  <p><cite>— Padre Pio to his spiritual director, on 5 August 1918, <em>Epistolario</em> (1995 ed.), 1065 (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>The experience lasted until 7 August. When Father Benedetto read his account, he recognized in it what the mystical tradition calls a transverberation, the piercing of the soul by divine love. Six weeks later, after his morning Mass, Padre Pio went up to the choir loft of the friary church to pray.</p>

<h2 id="the-morning-in-the-choir-loft">The Morning in the Choir Loft</h2>

<p>It was 20 September 1918. Mass was over, and Padre Pio had stayed behind to pray in the choir loft above the church of Our Lady of Grace. A month later, in a letter to Father Benedetto, he described what happened next. Something like a sleep came over him. He saw a figure before him. When it vanished, he found that his hands, his feet, and his side had been pierced, and that they were bleeding.</p>

<p>Eight years after the first red marks at Piana Romana, the wounds had come back, and this time they did not fade. He was thirty-one years old, and they would stay with him for the next fifty years, until the last months of his life. He covered his hands with mittens. But a priest’s hands are seen every morning at the altar, lifting the Host and the chalice. The young man who had begged in Pietrelcina for everything to stay hidden now carried the signs of the Passion in plain view.</p>

<p>He belonged to the family of St. Francis of Assisi, and Francis, centuries before, had been the first friar to bear such wounds. For Padre Pio they meant what his letters had been saying for years: that Calvary is the mountain of the saints, and that under the Cross one learns to love. They also meant pain, in the day and, as he would admit much later, even more at night. And from now on, everything he did would be watched.</p>

<p>Word of the stigmatized friar spread, and people began to make their way up to San Giovanni Rotondo. So did the doctors.</p>

<h2 id="the-doctors-who-came-to-look">The Doctors Who Came to Look</h2>

<p>The first careful examination came on 15 and 16 May 1919, when Dr. Luigi Romanelli, head surgeon at Barletta, studied the wounds. He returned again and again over the following year, five visits in all, and came away convinced that they could not be explained by natural causes. That summer a professor of pathology from Rome, Bignami, examined them and reached the opposite verdict. In his judgment they were real lesions, produced unconsciously by suggestion and kept open with iodine. In the autumn Dr. Giorgio Festa came, and he kept coming: again in July 1920 together with Romanelli, and again in 1925, when his report to the Holy Office was favorable. Festa also recorded, in 1920, a fever of 48 degrees Celsius, a reading that remains disputed. Three doctors had looked closely, and they could not agree.</p>

<p>In April 1920 came a visitor who did not look at all. Father Agostino Gemelli arrived at the friary and asked to examine the wounds. Padre Pio asked whether he had written authorization. Gemelli had none, and so Padre Pio would not show him. For the friar it was a matter of obedience: without an order, he would not uncover them. Gemelli left and wrote a severe judgment anyway, calling the wounds hysterical and saying they fitted the kind of sores a caustic substance would make. He had never examined them.</p>

<p>The following June the Holy Office sent its own man. Bishop Raffaello Carlo Rossi of Volterra arrived on 14 June 1921 as a secret visitor and stayed eight days. In the friar’s cell, he noted, there was a strong and pleasant fragrance, something like violets; he looked for its source and found only ordinary soap. Rossi also had to weigh an accusation. A pharmacist had testified that Padre Pio had obtained carbolic acid and veratrine. His explanation was simple: the acid was for sterilizing syringes, and the veratrine was for a joke on the brothers who took snuff. Rossi saw in it simplicity and a playful spirit.</p>

<p>The bishop asked about the stories of bilocation, too. Padre Pio told him that he did not know how it happened, whether it was his mind that was present somewhere else, or some image of him, or his body. Sometimes, he said, he could sense other people’s sins or virtues clearly. On 4 October 1921 Rossi sent the Holy Office his report. The stigmata, he concluded, were a real fact, neither fraud nor the work of the devil, while the many healings people claimed remained unconfirmed.</p>

<p>The report went into the files in Rome. It did not decide what happened next.</p>

<h2 id="the-town-that-kept-its-friar">The Town That Kept Its Friar</h2>

<p>The Holy Office was the Vatican body charged with guarding the faith. It took its first measures against Padre Pio in June 1922. On 31 May 1923 it issued its finding: “non constat de supernaturalitate,” it had not been established that the events surrounding him were supernatural. In mid-June he was ordered to say Mass only in the friary’s private chapel and to stop answering letters. On 5 July the decree was published in <em>L’Osservatore Romano</em>, the Vatican’s newspaper. It did not call him a fraud; it said only that nothing supernatural had been proved.</p>

<p>San Giovanni Rotondo refused to accept it. On 26 June the townspeople protested, and Padre Pio was allowed back to say Mass in the church. At the end of July an order came to move him to a friary at Ancona. The town rose up in agitation, and on 17 August the transfer was put off. He stayed where he was, and for the next eight years the decrees kept coming, each one declining to recognize any supernatural cause.</p>

<p>His mother spent Christmas of 1928 at San Giovanni Rotondo, in the house of Mary Pyle, and died there on 3 January 1929.</p>

<p>Then, in 1931, the Holy See took away everything but the altar. He was stripped of every priestly activity except Mass, and that he could say only in private. No crowds, no confessions. He said nothing in public in his own defense. Long before, in a letter, he had told a superior that he acted only to obey: “Opero solamente per ubbidirvi.” Now the sentence was put to the test.</p>

<p>The silence lasted two years. In July 1933 Pope Pius XI lifted the restrictions, and on 16 July Padre Pio said Mass in public again. In March 1934 he was allowed to hear men’s confessions, and in May women’s. The decrees themselves, some writers note, were never withdrawn. For the thirty-four years that remained to him, the confessional would take most of his days.</p>

<h2 id="the-line-at-the-confessional">The Line at the Confessional</h2>

<p>He heard confessions for as many as eighteen hours a day, by popular accounts, and one popular estimate puts the total over his life at some six hundred thousand. He did not always make it easy. Pilgrims sometimes met with what looked like harshness, John Paul II said many years later, and almost all of them came back for absolution.</p>

<p>Those who came to him for guidance he sent toward prayer, again and again. He taught in short sayings that were easy to carry home, and the Church still quotes them:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“In books we look for God; in prayer we find him. Prayer is the key that opens God’s heart.”</p>
  <p><em>“Nei libri cerchiamo Dio, nella preghiera Lo troviamo. La preghiera è la chiave che apre il cuore di Dio.”</em></p>
  <p><cite>— Padre Pio, as quoted in his biography by the Dicastery for the Causes of Saints (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>To his spiritual daughters he wrote about the Cross the way other people write about flowers. One letter reads:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Keep Jesus crucified in your heart, and all the crosses of the world will seem to you like roses.”</p>
  <p><em>“Tieni nel tuo cuore Gesù crocifisso e tutte le croci del mondo ti sembreranno rose.”</em></p>
  <p><cite>— Padre Pio, letter to a spiritual daughter, <em>Epistolario</em> III, p. 827 (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Of his own wounds he almost never spoke. Once, when McCaffery sympathized with him, he admitted that the days were not the worst of it. “It is the nights,” he said. If he let himself fall asleep, the pain of the wounds multiplied. When he wrote a few words on prayer cards for visitors, the effort of holding the pen showed in the way his chin jutted with each word, and afterward he laughed at his own “scrawls.”</p>

<p>McCaffery said that what filled his memories of Padre Pio, more than anything else, was humor. To a husband with a talkative wife, Padre Pio offered to take on fifty percent of her if the husband would keep the other fifty. When McCaffery mentioned his old rector, the friar answered in mock horror: “A Rector! God save us from all such!” Bishop Rossi had noticed the same playfulness back in 1921.</p>

<h2 id="a-monk-at-the-bedside">A Monk at the Bedside</h2>

<p>Some people came to San Giovanni Rotondo because of a story. McCaffery heard one on a train from Bologna to Rome, from a young farmer near Padua who employed some sixty people. Not long after the war, the farmer said, he had been badly hurt in an accident on his farm, and clots had lodged in both his lungs. The new drugs helped for a while. Then the clots came back worse than before, and he understood that he was dying. He prayed that God would let someone closer to Him than he was plead for him. Then he saw a bearded monk at his bedside, who leaned over, put a hand on his chest, smiled, and was gone.</p>

<p>He felt at once that he was cured, and his doctors, to their surprise, confirmed it. He told no one but his mother, for fear of being thought unbalanced. Months later, invited to lunch at a business acquaintance’s house in Padua, he saw a photograph on the wall and froze: it was the monk. His host told him who it was. He took the train south that same evening, and by five the next morning he was in the church at San Giovanni Rotondo for Padre Pio’s Mass. When the friar came out to the altar, the farmer had no doubt left. He made his confession to him that day. At the end of it, Padre Pio asked him, in the most ordinary voice, how his lungs were now.</p>

<p>On another train, this one bound for Foggia, McCaffery met an American woman who had come all the way from Hawaii to see Padre Pio and was now terrified to go through with it. She had already spent three weeks in a hotel in Rome, unable to make the last part of the trip. He promised to introduce her to Father Dominic, an American Capuchin at the friary who, McCaffery told her, had been cured of cancer through Padre Pio. As they found a taxi at Foggia, a friar from San Giovanni Rotondo stepped off the same train. It was Father Dominic. The next day she came hunting for McCaffery. Alone in her room, she had noticed a strong perfume, and she had brought none with her. When Father Dominic heard what time it had happened, he said that was just when he had spoken to Padre Pio about her. “Quick off the mark,” he said.</p>

<p>The stories multiplied. Father Dominic believed that Padre Pio had come to his own father’s deathbed in Milwaukee, as the old man had asked. Luigi Orione, a priest who was himself later declared a saint, reported seeing Padre Pio in St. Peter’s in Rome, far from his friary, at a ceremony for St. Thérèse of Lisieux. A later book tells of a little Sicilian girl, Gemma di Giorgi, born without pupils in her eyes, whose grandmother brought her to him in 1947. Afterward, the story goes, she could see the sea and a steamboat on it, and Padre Pio told the grandmother that the child could see. Each was told and retold, and the crowds kept growing.</p>

<p>Padre Pio would not explain any of it. Asked about bilocation, he had told Bishop Rossi that he did not know how it happened. Asked about the perfume people noticed around him, he called it “just sweets for the children.” And when the Jesuit wondered about his humility, he talked about a borrowed watch.</p>

<p>In 1947 a young Polish priest who was studying in Rome, Karol Wojtyla, made the trip to San Giovanni Rotondo to meet him. Fifty-two years later, as Pope John Paul II, he would remember the visit in St. Peter’s Square. A story spread later that Padre Pio had foretold the young priest’s election as pope. Some who knew them have affirmed it and others have denied it, and John Paul II himself denied it. By then the friar had already begun the work that would outlast him.</p>

<h2 id="a-home-for-the-relief-of-suffering">A Home for the Relief of Suffering</h2>

<p>San Giovanni Rotondo had lost its clinic in an earthquake. On 9 January 1940 Padre Pio founded a committee to build a hospital in its place. Two years later, on 17 February 1942, with Europe at war, Pope Pius XII asked Catholics everywhere to pray for peace. Padre Pio urged his spiritual children to answer by forming groups that would meet to pray together. These were the first of his prayer groups, and by the end of his life there would be about seven hundred of them.</p>

<p>His father died on 7 October 1946. The following spring, on 19 May 1947, construction began. On 5 May 1956 the Casa Sollievo della Sofferenza, the Home for the Relief of Suffering, was dedicated. John Paul II later said that Padre Pio wanted it to offer medicine that was warm and human, and that through charity like this God’s “ordinary miracles” take place.</p>

<p>To mark the opening, an international conference of medical specialists met at San Giovanni Rotondo, and Padre Pio received them at the friary. He thanked them shyly for coming, praised the dedication of their lives, and reminded them gently that they could care for souls as well as bodies. One of them, the heart specialist Paul Dudley White, kept pressing close with his camera to photograph the friar as he spoke, and on his way out he said, “And, Father, congratulations on your wounds!” McCaffery was in the room too. When his turn came to be introduced, Padre Pio asked him which part of the world he came from. “San Giovanni Rotondo,” McCaffery said, and the friar teased him about it afterward.</p>

<p>In 1957 Pius XII dispensed Padre Pio from his vow of poverty so that he could oversee the hospital. On 1 July 1959 the friary’s new church was dedicated. The hospital had brought money to San Giovanni Rotondo, and where some of it came from would itself be argued over; the historian Luzzatto later raised questions about several of the donations. With the money came new suspicion.</p>

<h2 id="under-suspicion-once-again">Under Suspicion Once Again</h2>

<p>Opponents began to allege that the funds were being misused. In 1960 Pope John XXIII sent Monsignor Maccari to San Giovanni Rotondo to investigate, and Maccari’s private notes from that June, published long afterward by the historian Luzzatto, show how troubled he was. Microphones were reportedly hidden in Padre Pio’s cell and confessional. The pope himself, in a private note, wrote of a “straw idol.”</p>

<p>The suspicions outlived him. In 2007 Luzzatto published a critical history of Padre Pio and Italian politics. Among other things, it set out the pharmacist’s testimony about the carbolic acid and the veratrine, which critics read as a sign that he had made or kept open the wounds himself. Bishop Rossi had heard the friar’s own explanation in 1921 and accepted it, and Gaeta and Tornielli wrote in answer to Luzzatto. A psychiatrist, Cancrini, has offered the opinion that Padre Pio had a histrionic disorder. The Church, for its part, examined the whole of his life in the long process that followed his death.</p>

<p>For Padre Pio himself, the second storm passed more quickly than the first. On 30 July 1964, under Pope Paul VI, he was given back full freedom in his ministry, and the accusations were set aside. In 1965 and 1966 he received permission to keep celebrating Mass in Latin and then to celebrate seated. He moved about in a wheelchair now.</p>

<p>And in his last months something else changed. Slowly, the wounds he had carried since 1918 began to disappear.</p>

<h2 id="have-you-said-mass-yet">Have You Said Mass Yet?</h2>

<p>On 22 September 1968, fifty years after the morning in the choir loft, Padre Pio celebrated a solemn Mass for the anniversary of the stigmata. He was eighty-one and very frail, and on the altar steps he nearly fell.</p>

<p>That night Padre Pellegrino, one of his brother friars, was with him. Padre Pio asked to make his confession. Afterward he renewed his religious profession, the vows he had made as a young man. He asked Padre Pellegrino to beg the community’s pardon for any offense he had ever given, and to pass on his blessing to his spiritual children. Then, in the middle of the night, he asked a strange question: “Have you said Mass yet?”</p>

<p>Then he got up. The old man who now went everywhere in a wheelchair walked to the friary terrace without it. Soon his strength gave out, and he had to be helped back to his cell. There he began to repeat, softly, “Gesù, Maria”: Jesus, Mary. When Padre Pellegrino started for the door to fetch the superior, Padre Pio told him firmly not to disturb anyone. Padre Pellegrino went anyway. Doctors came from the hospital, but at about half past two on the morning of 23 September 1968 Padre Pio died, wearing his habit, with his rosary in his hand. Dr. Gusso, one of the doctors at his side, said afterward that it was the most peaceful death he had ever witnessed.</p>

<p>The wounds were gone. They had been fading for months, and when the doctor examined his body he found no wound and no scar. On 26 September about a hundred thousand people came to his funeral. Not long before he died, he had allowed a BBC crew to film him at Mass, though not to interview him. Its producer, Mischa Scorer, later told McCaffery that the crew had set one lamp to shine straight onto the altar, and that on the morning of the filming every lamp worked except that one. The documentary went out under the title “A Very Private Man.”</p>

<h2 id="two-healings-and-a-pope">Two Healings and a Pope</h2>

<p>The first steps toward his cause were taken within weeks of his death. On 29 November 1982 Rome gave its clearance for the cause to begin, and the inquiry in the diocese ran from 1983 to 1990. Its work was declared valid on 7 December 1990. In June 1997 the Vatican’s theologians agreed unanimously that he had practiced the Christian virtues to a heroic degree, and on 18 December 1997 John Paul II confirmed their judgment and declared him Venerable.</p>

<p>The miracle for his beatification came from Salerno. Between 1 and 6 November 1995, Consiglia De Martino, a woman of that city, was healed of a ruptured thoracic duct without treatment. According to the account gathered for her case, a swelling had appeared under her left collarbone, scans showed the rupture and a large pool of lymph, and surgery was set for 3 November. A friar of San Giovanni Rotondo, Fra Modestino, prayed for her at Padre Pio’s tomb, and in those days a perfume associated with him was noticed. By the day of the operation the swelling was gone. The next morning, she said, she heard a voice tell her that she was healed, and on 6 November she went home without surgery. In April 1998 the Vatican’s medical board found the cure scientifically inexplicable, without a dissenting vote. The theologians agreed in June, the cardinals and bishops in October, and the decree on the miracle followed on 21 December 1998.</p>

<p>On 2 May 1999, in St. Peter’s Square, John Paul II declared Padre Pio blessed. The Capuchin friar, he said, had “astonished the world.” In his homily he spoke of the friar as someone he had known: “Quando ero studente qui a Roma, ebbi io stesso occasione di conoscerlo personalmente.” When I was a student here in Rome, I had the chance to meet him myself.</p>

<p>The second miracle happened in San Giovanni Rotondo itself. On 20 January 2000 Matteo Pio Colella, a boy of seven, fell ill with fulminant bacterial meningitis, and recovered. The account compiled for his case says that he was the son of a doctor at Casa Sollievo, that he went into septic shock and a coma, and that his heart stopped once. His mother prayed at the tomb while the friars said the rosary, and she later said that she saw a bearded friar lift up a child, and noticed the scent of flowers. When the boy woke, he said that an old man with a white beard and a brown habit had told him he would get well quickly, and he picked out Padre Pio from a photograph. The decree recognizing the miracle followed on 20 December 2001.</p>

<p>On 16 June 2002, again in St. Peter’s Square, John Paul II declared Padre Pio of Pietrelcina a saint. He called him “il Santo Frate del Gargano,” the friar-saint of the Gargano. And in his homily he let the friar describe himself, in his own words: “Sono un povero frate che prega.” I am a poor friar who prays. The pope quoted another of his sayings too, that prayer is the best weapon we have, a key that opens the heart of God. The Catholic Church keeps his feast on 23 September, the day he died.</p>

<p>Six years later, his tomb was opened.</p>

<h2 id="why-the-crowds-still-come">Why the Crowds Still Come</h2>

<p>The exhumation began after midnight on 3 March 2008. Archbishop D’Ambrosio reported that the body was in fair condition: the skull was partly skeletal, but the chin and the body were well preserved, and the hands looked as if they had just been manicured. No sign of the stigmata could be seen. For the public display a silicone mask was made of his face, modeled on a photograph from 1968. On 24 April 2008 Cardinal Saraiva Martins said Mass before about fifteen thousand people as the body was shown. The display was extended until September 2009. In April 2010 his remains were moved to a new crypt decorated in gold.</p>

<p>Popes kept coming too. Karol Wojtyla had returned to San Giovanni Rotondo in 1974, and he came back as John Paul II in 1987 for the centenary of Padre Pio’s birth. Benedict XVI visited the shrine in 2009, and Francis went to Pietrelcina and San Giovanni Rotondo in 2018. In February 2016, at Francis’s request, Padre Pio’s relics were carried in procession down the Via della Conciliazione into St. Peter’s Basilica, together with the relics of St. Leopold Mandic, for the Jubilee of Mercy. The friar who had spent his life in the confessional lay in St. Peter’s on Ash Wednesday, when that year’s Lent began.</p>

<p>Pietrelcina, by one estimate, now receives about two million pilgrims a year. The prayer groups he started during the war received statutes approved by the Vatican in 1986, and by 2013 there were some 3,300 of them, with about three million members in sixty countries.</p>

<p>Three years after his death, Pope Paul VI tried to explain the fame of this friar to the superiors of the Capuchin order. Padre Pio, he pointed out, had been no philosopher and no scholar, and he had no means of his own. So why had the world come to him?</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“He said Mass humbly, he heard confessions from morning to evening… He was a man of prayer and suffering.”</p>
  <p><em>“Diceva la Messa umilmente, confessava dal mattino alla sera… Era un uomo di preghiera e di sofferenza.”</em></p>
  <p><cite>— Pope Paul VI to the Capuchin superiors, 20 February 1971 (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>It was the same answer Padre Pio had given in his own way. As a young priest he had asked the parish priest of Pietrelcina to pray that the marks would be taken from him, and in the last months of his life they were. When his tomb was opened forty years later, the hands that had bled for half a century showed no wound at all. What remained in San Giovanni Rotondo were the things he had spent the gift on: an altar, a confessional, a hospital, and people who pray. The watch had gone back to its owner.</p>

<h2 id="a-note-on-the-sources">A Note on the Sources</h2>

<p>Padre Pio’s letters are still under copyright, and the full text of the <em>Epistolario</em> was not read for this account. His words are quoted in short passages, in our own English, from the Italian given by the Dicastery for the Causes of Saints, the papal homilies, and the Italian Wikiquote, which cites the 1995 edition. Those passages have not been checked against the printed volumes. His own account of 20 September 1918, in a letter of 22 October 1918, is summarized here and not quoted, because we could not read the Italian original. The sources disagree on several dates. The first marks are dated to 7 September 1910 in his later account and in the chronology of the Capuchins of San Giovanni Rotondo. His permanent stigmata date from 20 September 1918, and his first letter about the marks is dated 8 September 1911. His words to the parish priest of Pietrelcina are given in the Italian Wikipedia without a named source. Bignami’s examination is dated 26 July 1919 by two sources and 12 July by a third, and Festa’s first visit is given as 9 October and as 28 October 1919. The decree of 1931 is dated 23 May in the Italian sources, while an English summary gives 9 June. His final discharge from the army is dated March 1918 in the chronologies, though one passage of the Italian Wikipedia gives March 1917. One English source says he was first stigmatized while hearing confessions; this account follows the Italian sources that place it after Mass in the choir. Bishop Rossi’s observations come from accounts of the Holy Office files published by Castelli in 2011, which we read only in a devotional summary and an encyclopedia article. The 1960 investigation, the microphones, and John XXIII’s note are known from historians’ work that we read only in encyclopedia summaries. Luigi Orione’s report is dated to the beatification of Thérèse of Lisieux in April 1923 by one source and to 1925 by another. The stories of the Padua farmer, the woman from Hawaii, and Father Dominic come from McCaffery alone, who heard most of them from the people involved. The case details of the two approved miracles come from a devotional compilation; their dates follow the Dicastery for the Causes of Saints, and one English chronology gives other dates for the second miracle. A remark often attributed to Pius XI in 1933, and a prayer often attributed to Padre Pio, are left out because no source for them could be checked.</p>

<h2 id="how-this-was-researched">How This Life Was Researched</h2>

<p>This life draws on sixteen sources in Italian and English, two of them seen only in search summaries. They include the official biography and the dated record of the cause from the Dicastery for the Causes of Saints, and the homilies John Paul II gave at the beatification and the canonization. They also include the chronology kept by the Capuchins of San Giovanni Rotondo and the memoir of John McCaffery, who knew Padre Pio and wrote down what he saw. Padre Pio’s own words come from about twenty short passages of his letters and sayings, quoted in those sources. The positio of his cause, the decrees on his miracles, and the full text of his letters were not read. The translations from Italian are our own.</p>

